      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Carbon Capture and Utilization in Renewable Energy\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Carbon capture and utilization (CCU) technologies can be powered by renewable energy sources like solar and wind to reduce the carbon footprint of the capture process itself\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/carbon-capture-utilisation-and-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct air capture (DAC) powered by renewable electricity can extract CO2 directly from the atmosphere with current costs ranging from $100-$300 per ton\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.carbonbrief.org/direct-air-capture\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Captured carbon can be utilized in enhanced oil recovery, chemical production, building materials, and synthetic fuels, creating circular economy applications\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/energy-system/carbon-capture-utilisation-and-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Renewable energy-powered carbon capture facilities are operational or in development in countries including Norway, Switzerland, Canada, and the United States\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.climeworks.com\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Power-to-X technologies use excess renewable energy to convert captured CO2 into methane, methanol, or other chemicals, addressing intermittency challenges\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/articles/how-carbon-capture-can-support-hydrogen-economy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global carbon capture capacity reached approximately 10 megatons per year in 2023, with most projects operating in industrial applications rather than direct air capture\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.globalccsinstitute.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Global-Status-of-CCS-2023.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of electricity from renewables-powered CCU projects continues to decrease due to falling solar and wind costs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2023-May/Renewable-Energy-Costs-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
