      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Smart Grid and Energy Distribution\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Smart grids use digital communication technology and automated control systems to monitor and manage the transport of electricity from all generation sources to meet varying electricity demands in real-time\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/oe/smart-grid\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Smart grid technology enables two-way communication between utilities and customers, allowing for demand response programs that can reduce peak load and improve grid stability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/grid/smart-grid.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Advanced Metering Infrastructure (AMI) is a core component of smart grids that provides real-time or near-real-time consumption data to both utilities and consumers\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=41453\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Smart grids can integrate distributed energy resources including rooftop solar panels, wind turbines, and battery storage systems more efficiently than traditional grids\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/grid/distributed-energy.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Microgrids are localized smart grid systems that can operate independently from the main grid during outages, improving resilience and energy security\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/oe/microgrids\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Smart grid deployment requires significant cybersecurity measures to protect against cyberattacks that could disrupt electricity distribution and critical infrastructure\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nist.gov/publications/guidelines-smart-grid-cybersecurity\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Voltage and frequency regulation in smart grids can be optimized through automated controls and AI-based forecasting, reducing energy losses in distribution\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ieee.org/publications/transactions-smart-grid\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Smart grids can reduce peak demand by 10-20% through demand-side management and load shifting strategies when widely implemented\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/smart-grids\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Energy storage systems, particularly battery storage, are critical enabling technologies for smart grids to balance intermittent renewable energy generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy19osti/74192.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Smart grid infrastructure investment costs vary significantly by region, ranging from approximately $1-7 billion per utility depending on grid size and modernization scope\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032119305898\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
