      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Advanced Geothermal (Enhanced Geothermal Systems and Deep Direct Use)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS) involve creating artificial fractures in hot rock formations to circulate fluid, enabling geothermal energy generation in locations without natural hydrothermal reservoirs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/volcanic_geothermal_systems.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS requires hydraulic stimulation techniques to improve permeability and create connected pathways for fluid flow through crystalline basement rock\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/geothermal/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Deep Direct Use involves utilizing geothermal heat directly for heating and cooling applications at depths of 500-2000 meters, requiring less heat than traditional EGS power generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The Pangea project and similar commercial EGS ventures have demonstrated technical feasibility but face challenges with induced seismicity and fluid circulation optimization\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/geothermal/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS potential exists globally in areas with suitable geological conditions (hot rock at accessible depths), particularly in crystalline basement formations beneath sedimentary layers\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/where-are-geothermal-resources-located\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Capital costs for EGS projects remain high ($20-50 million+ for pilot projects), with operational costs dependent on successful long-term reservoir maintenance\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/geothermal/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Induced seismicity from fluid injection and extraction in EGS requires careful monitoring and management to ensure public safety and project viability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/events/induced-seismicity.php\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Deep Direct Use applications include district heating, greenhouse operations, and industrial process heat, offering lower temperature requirements than electricity generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/geothermal/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Technology maturity for EGS power generation remains at demonstration scale, with multiple international pilot projects operating or planned in Europe, Asia, and North America\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/geothermal/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Deep Direct Use systems typically operate at temperatures of 50-100\u00b0C, making them suitable for cascade utilization in combination with power generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
