      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Nuclear Fusion Energy\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Nuclear fusion is a process where two light atomic nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus, releasing enormous amounts of energy according to Einstein's mass-energy equivalence (E=mc\u00b2)\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainers-nuclear-fusion\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The most promising fusion reaction for energy production involves deuterium and tritium isotopes of hydrogen, which requires temperatures exceeding 100 million Kelvin to initiate\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iter.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"In December 2022, the National Ignition Facility (NIF) achieved net energy gain from a fusion reaction for the first time, producing more energy than was directly delivered to the fuel\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.llnl.gov/news/national-ignition-facility-achieves-fusion-ignition\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Magnetic confinement fusion (tokamaks and stellarators) and inertial confinement fusion (laser-based) are the two primary approaches being developed for commercial fusion energy\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/fusion\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Fusion energy produces no greenhouse gas emissions during operation and generates minimal long-lived radioactive waste compared to fission reactors\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.world-nuclear.org/information-library/current-and-future-generation/nuclear-fusion.aspx\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Commercial fusion power plants are still in development phases, with most projections suggesting viable commercial deployment between 2035-2050\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/fusion\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tritium, a key fuel component for D-T fusion reactions, is radioactive and scarce, requiring breeding within fusion reactor blankets as part of future reactor designs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iter.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Private fusion companies including Commonwealth Fusion Systems, TAE Technologies, and Helion Energy are pursuing alternative fusion approaches with significant venture capital funding\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainers-nuclear-fusion\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
