      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Geothermal Energy (Direct Use and Power Generation)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal energy provides baseload power with capacity factors typically ranging from 70-90%, significantly higher than solar (15-25%) and wind (25-35%) installations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79374.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct use applications of geothermal energy include district heating, greenhouse heating, aquaculture, and industrial processes, accounting for approximately 28% of global geothermal energy use\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ren21.net/gsr\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global geothermal electricity generation capacity reached approximately 14.1 GW in 2021, with Iceland, New Zealand, Indonesia, Mexico, and the Philippines being the leading producers\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2022-Oct/Geothermal-power-generation\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS) technology enables geothermal development in non-volcanic regions by artificially creating reservoirs, though commercial viability remains limited\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-enhanced-geothermal-system\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal heat pumps can provide heating and cooling with coefficients of performance (COP) of 3-6, meaning they deliver 3-6 units of thermal energy for each unit of electricity consumed\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energysavingtrust.org.uk/advice/heat-pumps/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal resources are geographically limited to tectonically active regions, with only about 10% of Earth's surface having economically viable conventional geothermal resources at shallow depths\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/geothermal-heat-and-electricity\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Induced seismicity from geothermal operations is a recognized concern, particularly with high-pressure injection and EGS projects, though modern monitoring can mitigate risks\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/volcanic-and-geothermal-hazards\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Levelized Cost of Electricity (LCOE) from conventional geothermal plants ranges from $60-100 per MWh in favorable locations, competitive with fossil fuels and renewables\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.lazard.com/research/levelized-cost-of-energy-and-levelized-cost-of-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct use geothermal applications can achieve energy efficiency of 50-90% depending on the application type and system design\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy13osti/59729.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Iceland generates approximately 30% of its electricity from geothermal sources and uses geothermal energy for district heating in over 90% of buildings\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.orkustofnun.is/gogn/simpair/2022_English.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
