      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydroelectric Power (Dam, Run-of-River, Pumped Storage)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydroelectric power accounts for approximately 16% of global electricity generation, making it the largest source of renewable electricity worldwide\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydropower-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Traditional dam-based hydroelectric systems store water in reservoirs and can generate electricity on demand, providing baseload power and grid stability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-hydroelectric-power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Run-of-river hydroelectric systems generate power from flowing water without large reservoirs, reducing environmental impact but offering less storage capacity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydropower\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Pumped-storage hydroelectricity works by pumping water uphill during low-demand periods and releasing it through turbines during peak demand, acting as a giant battery\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydropower-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Pumped storage facilities currently represent the largest share of energy storage capacity globally, accounting for over 90% of installed storage worldwide\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021-May/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydroelectric dams can have multi-purpose benefits including flood control, water supply, irrigation, and recreation in addition to electricity generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-hydroelectric-power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydroelectric facilities have long operational lifespans of 50-100+ years with minimal ongoing fuel costs once constructed\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydropower\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns with large dams include habitat disruption, fish migration barriers, and methane emissions from reservoir decomposition in tropical regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.worldwildlife.org/threats/barriers-to-fish-migration\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Run-of-river systems typically have lower environmental impact than traditional dams but still require careful water flow management and fish passage considerations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydropower\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"China is the world's largest hydroelectric power producer, followed by Canada, Brazil, and the United States\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydropower-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
