      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Synthetic Fuels and Power-to-X Technologies\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Power-to-X (P2X) technologies convert renewable electricity into chemical energy carriers such as synthetic fuels, hydrogen, and chemicals through electrolysis and chemical synthesis processes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022/Jan/Renewable-Electricity-Powered-Hydrogen-and-Synthetic-Fuels\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Synthetic fuels (e-fuels) are produced by combining hydrogen generated from renewable electricity with captured carbon dioxide, enabling carbon-neutral liquid fuels for transportation and industry\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/sustainable-aviation-fuels\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The production cost of green hydrogen through electrolysis using renewable electricity has declined by approximately 30% between 2015 and 2021\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022/Jun/Hydrogen-from-Renewable-Power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Power-to-X technologies are particularly valuable for hard-to-decarbonize sectors including aviation, shipping, and high-temperature industrial processes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/chemicals-and-materials/our-insights/how-green-hydrogen-is-becoming-a-key-pillar-of-decarbonization\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The global hydrogen production currently stands at approximately 120 million tonnes annually, but over 95% is produced from fossil fuels; green hydrogen represents less than 1% of total production\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct air capture (DAC) combined with green hydrogen can produce synthetic fuels with carbon negativity when renewable electricity is used, addressing both emissions reduction and energy storage\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.carbon-direct.com/synthetic-fuels\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The European Union has set targets to produce 10 million tonnes of renewable hydrogen by 2030 as part of its REPowerEU plan to reduce fossil fuel dependence\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://ec.europa.eu/growth/articles/repowereu-action-support-affordable-secure-and-sustainable-energy_en\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Alkaline electrolysis and proton exchange membrane (PEM) electrolysis are the most mature technologies for green hydrogen production, with PEM systems achieving higher efficiency and flexibility\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/hydrogen/electrolysis.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Power-to-ammonia (P2A) is emerging as a significant Power-to-X pathway, allowing renewable hydrogen to be converted into ammonia for use as a carbon-free fuel and fertilizer feedstock\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The round-trip efficiency of Power-to-X technologies currently ranges from 40-60% depending on the conversion pathway, with hydrogen production via PEM electrolysis achieving approximately 70% efficiency\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2020/Dec/Hydrogen-A-renewable-energy-perspective\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
