  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Ocean Energy (Tidal, Wave, Osmotic)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy is the most mature ocean energy technology, with predictable tidal cycles occurring twice daily and energy density approximately 800 times greater than wind energy at the same velocity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global tidal stream energy potential is estimated at approximately 1,000 TWh/year, with the most economically viable sites located in coastal areas with high tidal ranges such as the Bay of Fundy and the UK's Severn Estuary\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy has a theoretical global potential of approximately 2-3 TW, with the highest wave energy resources found in stormy ocean regions between 30-60 degrees latitude in both hemispheres\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy converters are significantly less developed than tidal devices, with limited commercial-scale deployments and challenges related to survivability in extreme sea conditions and power take-off system reliability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Osmotic energy (salinity gradient energy) exploits the osmotic pressure difference between fresh and salt water, with a theoretical potential of approximately 2 TW globally at river mouths and estuaries\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Osmotic energy technology remains in early pilot stages with the Statkraft osmotic power plant prototype in Norway (2009-2013) being one of the few demonstration projects, and commercial viability is uncertain\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns for ocean energy include impacts on marine wildlife migration patterns, habitat disruption, collision risks with tidal/wave devices, and electromagnetic field effects from subsea cables\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current tidal energy deployment costs range from $4-6 million per MW, making tidal energy more expensive than offshore wind but less expensive than most wave energy systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal barrage systems, which dam estuaries to capture tidal water flow, have environmental concerns related to sediment transport and ecosystem impacts but offer higher energy density than tidal stream devices\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Europe leads global ocean energy development, with the UK, France, and Scandinavian countries hosting the majority of pilot and prototype projects and receiving substantial government research funding\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Oct/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
