  "container": null,
  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Floating and Offshore Wind Technology\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating offshore wind turbines can be deployed in water depths greater than 60 meters, allowing access to stronger and more consistent wind resources compared to fixed-bottom installations limited to shallower waters\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/78773.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The three primary floating platform designs are spar buoys, tension-leg platforms (TLP), and semi-submersibles, each with distinct mooring and stability characteristics\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0360544220304509\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global floating offshore wind capacity reached approximately 0.5 GW by 2022, with projects primarily concentrated in Europe, particularly in the North Sea and Mediterranean regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://gwec.net/global-wind-report-2023/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating offshore wind technology is expected to reduce levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) from current estimates of $150-200/MWh to $70-100/MWh by 2030-2035 through economies of scale and technological improvements\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021-Jun/Renewable-power-generation-costs-in-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Mooring systems represent 10-15% of total floating offshore wind platform costs and are critical design factors affecting installation and operational reliability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/75400.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating platforms typically operate with 8-12 year design lifespans for initial deployments, with next-generation designs targeting 25-30 year operational lifespans matching traditional fixed-bottom offshore turbines\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0960148120313100\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental and regulatory challenges for floating offshore wind include bird and marine mammal interactions, electromagnetic field effects from subsea cables, and cumulative impacts assessment methodologies still under development\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.boem.gov/sites/default/files/documents/newsroom/Offshore-Wind-and-Fisheries-White-Paper-Final.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hywind Scotland, commissioned in 2017, was the world's first commercial floating offshore wind farm and established technical feasibility with five 6 MW turbines operating in 100+ meter water depths\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.equinor.com/en/what-we-do/hywind.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Supply chain constraints for floating platform construction, specialized installation vessels, and skilled workforce availability currently limit deployment rates and represent major commercialization barriers\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy-industry-review.com/analysis/floating-offshore-wind/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating offshore wind resources in deep water (>200m) could theoretically supply 4x current global electricity demand, particularly in regions like the Pacific Coast of North America and deep Atlantic waters\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/78773.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
