      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Residential and Distributed Generation\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Distributed Generation (DG) refers to small-scale power generation facilities (typically 1 kW to 10 MW) located close to end-users, reducing transmission losses and improving grid resilience compared to centralized generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy04osti/35964.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Residential solar photovoltaic (PV) systems are the most common form of distributed generation in developed countries, with capacity growing at 15-20% annually in recent years\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/solar-pv\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Battery energy storage systems paired with residential solar installations improve self-consumption rates from approximately 30% to 70-80%, enhancing economic viability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy17osti/67921.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Residential wind turbines (10-50 kW capacity) are viable in locations with average annual wind speeds exceeding 10 mph, but face adoption barriers including zoning restrictions and noise concerns\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://windexchange.energy.gov/small-wind\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Net metering policies allow residential generators to send excess electricity to the grid and receive credits, significantly improving project economics, though policy frameworks vary substantially by jurisdiction\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ncsl.org/energy-environment-and-transportation/net-metering-policy-overview\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Microgrids integrating distributed generation with energy storage and smart controls can operate autonomously during grid outages, improving community resilience\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/articles/what-microgrid\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Combined Heat and Power (CHP) systems in residential applications achieve 60-80% overall efficiency compared to 45-50% for conventional separate heat and power generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/combined-heat-and-power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal heat pumps for residential heating and cooling represent distributed thermal generation with Coefficient of Performance (COP) values of 3-5, though initial capital costs are 25-50% higher than air-source systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal-heat-pumps\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Grid integration challenges from distributed generation include voltage regulation, frequency stability, and phase imbalances, requiring investment in smart inverters and grid modernization infrastructure\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy18osti/71284.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Residential solar costs have declined 89% from 2010 to 2020, with installed system prices dropping from approximately $7.86/Watt to $2.71/Watt in the US\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80694.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
