  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Wind Energy (Onshore and Offshore)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind power is the most cost-effective renewable energy technology, with levelized costs of electricity (LCOE) ranging from $26-50/MWh as of 2021, making it competitive with fossil fuels in most markets\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022-Jul/Renewable-Power-Generation-Costs-in-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind capacity globally reached approximately 54 GW in 2021, with most capacity concentrated in Europe (around 25 GW) and China (around 16 GW)\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://gwec.net/global-wind-report-2022/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Modern utility-scale wind turbines range from 2-12 MW for onshore installations and 12-15 MW for offshore installations, with capacity factors typically between 35-45% onshore and 50-60% offshore\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80694.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind installations have higher capital costs of $3,000-4,000/kW compared to onshore costs of $1,200-1,600/kW, but generate higher annual energy yields due to stronger and more consistent winds\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2020-Jun/Offshore-Wind-Outlook-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy avoided approximately 600 million metric tons of CO2 emissions globally in 2020, equivalent to removing 130 million vehicles from roads for one year\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://gwec.net/global-wind-report-2021/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind projects typically take 2-4 years from planning to operation, while offshore projects take 4-6 years due to environmental assessments, permitting, and marine logistics complexity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy18osti/71284.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind turbine blade recycling and end-of-life management remains a challenge, with most current practice involving landfill disposal, though composites recycling technology is under active development\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy19osti/73524.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating offshore wind technology is emerging as viable for deep-water installations beyond 60 meters depth, with first commercial projects operational in Scotland and Norway\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022-Jul/Floating-offshore-wind-Blowing-in-new-wind-\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Grid integration challenges include wind intermittency requiring battery storage or backup capacity, with typical requirements of 15-30% additional system flexibility per 30% wind penetration\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/75475.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Land use efficiency for onshore wind is high at approximately 2-4 hectares per MW, with additional land available for agriculture or other uses beneath turbines\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41560-017-0004-1\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
