      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Floating and rooftop solar installations\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating solar (floatovoltaics) are photovoltaic systems installed on water bodies such as reservoirs, lakes, and ponds, offering space efficiency and reduced evaporation benefits\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80325.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Rooftop solar installations are the most common distributed solar application, typically installed on residential and commercial building roofs to generate electricity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/rooftop-solar\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating solar systems can reduce water evaporation by 50-90% compared to open water bodies, providing dual benefits for water conservation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022/Aug/Innovation-landscape-brief-Floating-solar\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Rooftop solar installations avoid land-use conflicts and can reduce building cooling costs by 5-10% through shading effects\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy19osti/73531.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating solar installations typically have lower albedo-related losses and benefit from water cooling effects, improving efficiency by 5-15% compared to ground-mounted systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80325.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global floating solar capacity reached approximately 4 GW by 2021, with significant growth potential in Asia-Pacific regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022/Aug/Innovation-landscape-brief-Floating-solar\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Installation and maintenance costs for floating solar are typically 10-30% higher than ground-mounted systems due to specialized equipment and anchoring requirements\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80325.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Rooftop solar installations face challenges including structural requirements, shading from surrounding buildings, and roof orientation limitations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/rooftop-solar\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns for floating solar include potential impacts on aquatic ecosystems, phytoplankton dynamics, and fish migration patterns\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80325.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Net metering policies have significantly increased rooftop solar adoption by allowing homeowners to sell excess electricity back to the grid\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/net-metering\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
