      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Radioisotope thermoelectric generators\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTGs) convert heat from radioactive decay into electricity using the Seebeck effect in thermoelectric materials, without moving parts or external fuel requirements.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nasa.gov/news/radioisotope-power-systems/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Plutonium-238 has historically been the primary fuel for RTGs due to its 87.7-year half-life, high specific power output, and low neutron and gamma radiation, though production has been limited since the 1980s.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1762451\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"NASA has successfully deployed RTGs on multiple deep space missions including Voyager 1 and 2, Cassini, and Curiosity rover, where solar power is insufficient due to distance from the sun.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/missions/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"RTGs achieve conversion efficiency of approximately 5-8%, significantly lower than conventional thermal power plants but acceptable for applications where reliability and longevity outweigh efficiency concerns.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/radioisotope-power-systems-deep-space-exploration\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"RTGs have operational lifespans of 20-40+ years depending on the isotope and mission requirements, making them suitable for long-duration space missions where maintenance is impossible.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1762451\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current RTG development programs focus on Americium-241 and polonium-210 as alternatives to Plutonium-238 due to supply constraints and non-proliferation concerns.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/radioisotope-power-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"RTGs have minimal terrestrial applications beyond niche uses in remote research stations and lighthouses due to regulatory restrictions, proliferation concerns, and the availability of alternative power sources.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.world-nuclear.org/information-library/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"RTGs are not considered renewable energy in the traditional sense since they rely on finite radioisotope reserves, though they represent non-combustion power generation for space applications.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/1762451\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
