      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Thermoacoustic and Stirling engines\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Stirling engines are external combustion engines that operate on a regenerative thermodynamic cycle, using temperature differences to produce mechanical work without internal combustion\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stirling_engine\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Thermoacoustic engines convert heat directly into acoustic work or oscillating flow without moving pistons or turbines, using pressure and velocity oscillations in a gas\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermoacoustic_engine\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Stirling engines can achieve theoretical thermal efficiencies approaching the Carnot efficiency limit, though practical engines typically achieve 30-40% efficiency\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/stirling-engines\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Thermoacoustic engines have no moving parts in the working gas, making them potentially more reliable and lower maintenance than conventional engines\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/thermoacoustic-engine\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Stirling engines can operate with any external heat source, including solar thermal, geothermal, or waste heat from industrial processes, making them suitable for renewable energy applications\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stirling_engine#Applications\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Thermoacoustic engines are being researched for applications in waste heat recovery, solar energy conversion, and cryogenic cooling systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/331476857_Thermoacoustic_engines_and_refrigerators\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Stirling engine development challenges include sealing issues at high temperatures, slow startup times, and difficulty scaling to large power outputs compared to internal combustion engines\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stirling_engine#Disadvantages\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Recent commercialization efforts for Stirling engines include solar power generation systems and combined heat and power units for residential applications\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy04osti/35575.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Thermoacoustic technology operates without mechanical pistons or turbines, reducing vibration and enabling compact designs suitable for distributed energy applications\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/thermoacoustic-engine\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Both Stirling and thermoacoustic engines can theoretically achieve higher thermal efficiencies than conventional steam turbines for small-scale renewable energy systems operating with limited temperature differences\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/13/12/3279\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
