      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Carbon capture and renewable integration\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct Air Capture (DAC) technology can be powered by renewable energy sources, enabling carbon-negative electricity generation when integrated with wind and solar facilities\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Point-source carbon capture from industrial facilities can be integrated with renewable hydrogen production to create carbon-neutral synthetic fuels\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80472.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Renewable-powered carbon capture requires significant electrical input, with DAC systems consuming approximately 200-600 kWh of electricity per ton of CO2 captured depending on technology type\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/direct-air-capture\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Integration of carbon capture with concentrated solar power (CSP) systems can utilize waste heat to reduce energy requirements for CO2 capture processes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41560-019-0402-6\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Renewable energy integration with carbon capture enables circular economy applications including enhanced oil recovery, building materials production, and chemical synthesis\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.carbonbrief.org/carbon-capture\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid systems combining renewable electricity with carbon capture show economic viability primarily in regions with cheap renewable energy and existing industrial infrastructure\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/ccus-in-clean-energy-transitions\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Grid-scale energy storage systems may be necessary to match intermittent renewable power generation with continuous carbon capture operations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/77073.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Renewable-powered bioenergy with carbon capture and storage (BECCS) represents a negative emissions technology pathway for decarbonization targets\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
