      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Advanced geothermal (enhanced geothermal systems)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS) are engineered reservoirs created in hot rock formations where natural permeability is insufficient, using hydraulic stimulation to increase rock permeability and enable fluid circulation for heat extraction\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal/enhanced-geothermal-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The Krafla Magma Testbed in Iceland successfully drilled into magma at 400\u00b0C in 2021, representing a breakthrough in accessing ultra-high temperature geothermal resources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.islandsmidja.is/en/krafla-magma-testbed/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Fervo Energy completed the first commercial EGS project demonstration in Nevada in 2023, showing the viability of EGS technology for utility-scale power generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.fervoenergy.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS technology could potentially expand geothermal energy production to regions without traditional high-heat-flow geothermal resources, significantly increasing the geographic potential for geothermal power\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal/enhanced-geothermal-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The U.S. Department of Energy invested approximately $120 million in EGS research and development programs through various funding initiatives as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/articles/department-energy-announces-funding-enhanced-geothermal-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Technical challenges in EGS include induced seismicity from hydraulic stimulation, maintaining long-term fluid circulation without permeability degradation, and accurately characterizing subsurface conditions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"EGS systems have thermal energy potential equivalent to multiple times global energy consumption, with most accessible resources located at depths of 3-10 kilometers\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal/enhanced-geothermal-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Commonwealth Fusion Systems and MIT are developing supercritical geothermal wells targeting depths around 5 kilometers where fluids reach supercritical states, potentially increasing power output by 5-10 times compared to conventional geothermal\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.commonwealthfusion.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
