      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydrogen fuel and fuel cells\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen fuel cells generate electricity through an electrochemical reaction between hydrogen and oxygen, producing only water and heat as byproducts, making them zero-emission at point of use\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Proton Exchange Membrane (PEM) fuel cells are the most developed technology for transportation applications and operate at relatively low temperatures (around 80\u00b0C)\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.fuelcellsworks.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current hydrogen production is primarily from natural gas reforming (approximately 95%), which generates carbon dioxide emissions, limiting the environmental benefits unless coupled with carbon capture\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Green hydrogen produced through electrolysis powered by renewable electricity represents less than 1% of current hydrogen production globally\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Fuel cell vehicles have a driving range comparable to gasoline vehicles (300-400 miles) and refueling times of 3-5 minutes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen storage and distribution infrastructure remains limited, with fewer than 10,000 publicly available hydrogen refueling stations globally, primarily concentrated in Japan, Germany, and California\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Fuel cells have applications beyond vehicles, including stationary power generation, backup power systems, and industrial heat processes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen has a volumetric energy density significantly lower than gasoline, requiring either high-pressure tanks (350-700 bar) or cryogenic storage at -253\u00b0C, both presenting engineering challenges\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Solid Oxide Fuel Cells (SOFCs) and Molten Carbonate Fuel Cells (MCFCs) operate at high temperatures (600-1000\u00b0C) and are better suited for stationary applications requiring combined heat and power\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The International Energy Agency projects hydrogen could account for up to 10-15% of global energy demand by 2050 if current policy and investment trends continue\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Durability and cost remain major challenges, with fuel cell stack degradation occurring over time and current system costs ranging from $3,000-$8,000 per kilowatt\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen can be produced through renewable methods including electrolysis, thermochemical water splitting, and biological processes, though these methods currently require further development for cost competitiveness\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
