      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Nuclear fusion (experimental renewable alternative)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The National Ignition Facility (NIF) achieved net energy gain from nuclear fusion in December 2022, producing more energy output than was directly delivered to the fuel, marking the first time this has been accomplished in a controlled laboratory setting.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/articles/national-ignition-facility-achieves-fusion-ignition\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Nuclear fusion works by combining two light atomic nuclei to form a heavier nucleus, releasing large amounts of energy in the process, and is considered a clean energy source as it produces no greenhouse gases or long-lived radioactive waste.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iaea.org/topics/fusion-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"ITER (International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor) in France is the world's largest experimental fusion project, designed to demonstrate that fusion energy is scientifically and technologically feasible for commercial energy production.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iter.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Commonwealth Fusion Systems (CCS), a private company backed by MIT, announced plans to build SPARC, a demonstration fusion power plant targeting operations in the early 2030s using high-temperature superconductor magnets.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.commonwealthfusion.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current experimental fusion reactors require extreme conditions including temperatures exceeding 100 million degrees Celsius to sustain the fusion reaction, which remains a significant engineering challenge.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.britannica.com/technology/nuclear-fusion\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Commercial fusion energy is not yet available for electricity generation on the grid, and most experts estimate it will take at least 10-20 years before demonstration plants can prove viability.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/fusion-energy-is-one-of-many-options-to-decarbonise-power-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Deuterium-tritium fusion reactions are the most studied approach in major experimental projects like ITER and NIF because they have the highest reaction rates at achievable temperatures.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.world-nuclear.org/information-library/energy-and-the-environment/fusion-power.aspx\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
