      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hybrid renewable systems\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid renewable systems combine two or more renewable energy sources (such as solar, wind, hydro, and battery storage) to provide more reliable and consistent power generation by compensating for the intermittency of individual sources.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79816.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Solar-wind hybrid systems are among the most common configurations, as solar generation peaks during midday while wind generation often peaks at night and during cloudy periods, creating complementary generation patterns.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0960148121010916\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Battery energy storage systems (BESS) are increasingly integrated into hybrid renewable systems to store excess energy during peak generation and discharge it during periods of low generation or high demand.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/electricity-2024\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid renewable systems can reduce the levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) compared to single-source renewable systems by optimizing the capacity factor and reducing energy storage requirements.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022/Jul/Renewable-power-generation-costs-in-2022\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global capacity of hybrid renewable energy systems is growing rapidly, with over 1 GW of new hybrid renewable capacity added annually in recent years, particularly in India, Europe, and the United States.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/grid-integration-of-solar-photovoltaic-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid systems require advanced power management and control systems to coordinate the different renewable sources and optimize energy dispatch, which is a key technical challenge in implementation.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/publications\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen production through electrolysis powered by hybrid renewable systems is emerging as a potential application for seasonal energy storage and decarbonization of hard-to-abate sectors.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid renewable microgrids serving remote communities and industrial applications demonstrate improved reliability and resilience compared to single-source renewable systems or diesel-only systems.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy22osti/82063.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
