      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Wind energy (onshore and offshore)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy is the second-largest source of renewable electricity globally after hydropower, accounting for approximately 7-8% of global electricity generation as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2024-Mar/Renewable-capacity-statistics-2024\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind turbines typically have capacity factors of 25-35%, while offshore wind turbines achieve higher capacity factors of 40-50% due to stronger and more consistent wind resources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80364.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Modern onshore wind turbines have rotor diameters of 100-220 meters with hub heights of 80-220 meters, while offshore turbines are increasingly larger with capacities reaching 12-15 MW or higher\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2023-Apr/Wind-power-technology-brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) from onshore wind has decreased by approximately 70% over the past decade, making it one of the cheapest electricity sources in many regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2023-Jun/Renewable-power-generation-costs-in-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind deployment requires significant infrastructure including offshore substations, export cables, and specialized installation vessels, contributing to higher capital costs (2-3 times higher than onshore)\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy22osti/81747.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy installations avoid approximately 4 metric tons of CO2 emissions per megawatt-hour of electricity generated compared to coal-fired power plants\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy19osti/73341.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Europe leads global offshore wind deployment with over 5 GW of installed capacity, primarily in the North Sea, while Asia-Pacific is expanding rapidly with significant growth in China and Taiwan\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/offshore-wind-outlook-2024\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind turbine blade recycling remains a challenge, with most current blades ending up in landfills, though new technologies for composite material recycling are under development\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79869.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind farms typically require 5-10 acres per megawatt of capacity, allowing for dual land-use with agriculture or grazing in many cases\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14osti/61471.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Bird and bat mortality from wind turbines is estimated at 100,000-600,000 birds and 100,000-400,000 bats annually in the United States, though this remains small relative to other anthropogenic causes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/how-many-birds-are-killed-wind-turbines\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
