      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydroelectric and tidal power\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydroelectric power is the world's largest source of renewable electricity, accounting for approximately 16% of global electricity generation as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydropower-and-other-renewable-sources\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Norway generates approximately 96% of its electricity from hydroelectric power, the highest percentage of any country\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.statista.com/statistics/264485/electricity-generation-from-hydroelectric-power-by-country/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal power installations typically operate at a capacity factor of 40-50%, which is higher than wind or solar\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021-Feb/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The UK operates the largest tidal stream generator in the world, the MeyGen turbine in Scotland with a capacity of 6 MW\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.meygen.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Pumped-storage hydroelectricity is the most widely deployed energy storage technology globally, accounting for about 90% of grid-scale energy storage\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydro-power-and-the-missing-link-to-clean-electricity\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal power is still in early commercial stages with global installed capacity of approximately 0.5 GW as of 2024\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/statistics/renewable-capacity-statistics\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Modern hydroelectric dams can be designed with fish passage systems to mitigate environmental impacts on aquatic species\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-are-environmental-impacts-hydroelectric-power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"China is the world's largest hydroelectric power producer, generating over 1,300 TWh annually\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.statista.com/chart/23887/hydropower-generation-by-country/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal barrages, such as the Rance Tidal Power Station in France, can generate electricity from both incoming and outgoing tides\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.world-nuclear.org/information-library/energy-and-the-environment/renewable-energy-and-electricity.aspx\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Run-of-river hydroelectric systems require minimal water storage and have lower environmental impact compared to traditional dam reservoirs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.renewableenergyworld.com/articles/2020/jul/run-of-river-hydropower.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
