  "container": null,
  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Microgrid and distributed generation\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Microgrids are localized electrical systems that can operate independently or in conjunction with the main grid, typically powered by distributed generation sources including renewable energy, battery storage, and backup generators.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy13osti/57563.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Distributed generation refers to small-scale power generation units (typically 1 kW to 10 MW) located near the point of consumption, including solar photovoltaic systems, wind turbines, fuel cells, and microturbines.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ieee.org/publications_standards/publications/periodicals/power_energy_magazine.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Microgrids improve resilience by enabling islanding capability, allowing continued operation during main grid outages and reducing vulnerability to cascading failures.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy16osti/65033.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Key technical challenges for microgrids include voltage and frequency regulation, power flow control, protection coordination, and synchronization with the main grid during seamless transitions between grid-connected and islanded modes.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2018.08.009\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Distributed generation can reduce transmission and distribution losses by 3-5% compared to centralized generation systems due to proximity to load centers.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/distributed-generation\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Energy management systems and advanced controls, including demand response and battery energy storage systems (BESS), are essential components for optimizing microgrid performance and economic operation.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy19osti/73853.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Regulatory frameworks vary significantly by region; many jurisdictions require specific interconnection standards, such as IEEE 1547 in North America, to govern how distributed generation connects to the grid.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://standards.ieee.org/standard/1547-2018.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Microgrids are increasingly deployed in remote areas, industrial facilities, university campuses, and military bases to improve energy independence and reduce operational costs.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14osti/61143.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Inverter-based resources in microgrids with high renewable penetration present control challenges due to reduced system inertia compared to synchronous generators.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSYST.2019.2955123\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Economic viability of microgrids depends on factors including local electricity prices, availability of renewable resources, demand patterns, and incentive programs such as tax credits and renewable energy standards.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy18osti/71839.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
