      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Piezoelectric and kinetic energy harvesting\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric energy harvesting converts mechanical stress and vibration into electrical energy through the piezoelectric effect, where certain materials generate electrical charge when deformed\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032121003361\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Kinetic energy harvesting devices can capture energy from human motion, vehicular movement, and environmental vibrations to power wearable devices and sensors\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41560-021-00830-9\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric materials commonly used include lead zirconate titanate (PZT), polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF), and zinc oxide nanowires due to their high electromechanical coupling coefficients\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1944/14/13/3714\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Typical power output from piezoelectric energy harvesting ranges from microwatts to milliwatts depending on application, material, and vibration frequency\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8411098\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Footstep energy harvesting using piezoelectric tiles can generate 5-10 watts per step, with potential applications in high-traffic areas like airports and train stations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S136403211930326X\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Electromagnetic and electrostatic energy harvesting are alternative kinetic energy conversion methods that can achieve higher power outputs compared to piezoelectric alone in some applications\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032118307524\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hybrid energy harvesting systems combining piezoelectric and electromagnetic methods can improve overall efficiency and expand the frequency range of energy capture\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1944/13/5/1223\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Challenges in piezoelectric energy harvesting include impedance matching, low power density, and material brittleness, which researchers address through flexible polymers and nanostructured materials\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0960148120310369\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Recent advances include self-powered wearable sensors, smart clothing, and implantable medical devices powered by body motion and ambient vibrations\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-21511-6\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Lead-free piezoelectric materials such as sodium potassium niobate (KNN) and bismuth ferrite (BFO) are being developed to address environmental and health concerns associated with lead-based ceramics\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/10/18/6327\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
