  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Concentrated Solar Power (CSP)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP systems use mirrors or lenses to concentrate sunlight onto a small area, converting solar energy into heat that drives a turbine or engine to generate electricity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14osti/61514.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"There are four main CSP technologies: parabolic troughs, solar towers (central receivers), linear Fresnel reflectors, and dish/Stirling systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2018/Mar/Concentrating-Solar-Power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP plants can integrate thermal energy storage systems, typically using molten salt, allowing electricity generation after sunset and improving capacity factors to 50-75%\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy17osti/68485.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Parabolic trough technology is the most commercially deployed CSP technology globally, accounting for approximately 70% of installed CSP capacity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2016/Jun/Solar-thermal-electricity\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global CSP capacity was approximately 6.3 GW as of 2023, with significant installations in Spain, Morocco, South Africa, Chile, China, and the United States\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/PUBLICATIONS/2024/Jan/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2024\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP plants require direct normal irradiance (DNI) of at least 1800-2000 kWh/m\u00b2/year and are most viable in regions between 35\u00b0N and 35\u00b0S latitude with arid conditions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14osti/61514.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Levelized Cost of Electricity (LCOE) for CSP has declined from approximately $300-400/MWh in 2010 to $60-150/MWh by 2020, with further reductions expected\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021/Jun/Renewable-Cost-Database\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP requires significant water for cooling and cleaning of mirrors, typically 3-4 m\u00b3/MWh, making water availability a critical constraint in deployment regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14osti/61514.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Solar tower (central receiver) systems achieve higher operating temperatures (up to 900\u00b0C) compared to parabolic troughs (400\u00b0C), enabling higher thermodynamic efficiency and better thermal storage integration\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2018/Mar/Concentrating-Solar-Power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP plants have a typical lifespan of 25-30 years and land requirements of approximately 5-8 hectares per MW of installed capacity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14osti/61514.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
