  "container": null,
  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Renewable energy policy and incentives\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Feed-in tariffs (FITs) guarantee renewable energy producers fixed prices for electricity fed into the grid, creating long-term revenue certainty that has significantly accelerated renewable energy deployment in countries like Germany and Denmark\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2015/Jun/Renewable-Energy-Policy-Brief-Series\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Investment tax credits (ITCs) reduce the upfront cost of renewable energy projects by allowing investors to deduct a percentage of installation costs from their tax liability, with the U.S. federal solar ITC initially set at 30% and scheduled to phase down\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/savings/business-energy-investment-tax-credit-itc\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Renewable Portfolio Standards (RPS) mandate that utilities generate a specified percentage of electricity from renewable sources by a target date, driving market demand and technology innovation across multiple U.S. states and other jurisdictions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/news/program/2018/renewable-portfolio-standards.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Production tax credits (PTCs) provide tax incentives per unit of renewable electricity generated over a specified period, with wind energy PTCs in the U.S. historically ranging from $0.02-0.04 per kilowatt-hour\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.eia.gov/analysis/requests/subsidy/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Auction-based mechanisms for renewable energy contracts have increasingly replaced fixed incentive schemes, driving down costs through competitive bidding while ensuring projects achieve financial viability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2017/Dec/Renewable-Cost-Competitiveness\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Net metering policies allow distributed renewable energy generators (particularly rooftop solar) to receive credit for excess electricity supplied to the grid at the retail electricity rate, significantly improving project economics\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy16osti/65023.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Accelerated depreciation schedules reduce the effective cost of renewable energy investments by allowing faster write-offs, making projects more financially attractive to private investors\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2016/Jan/Renewable-Energy-Policy-Database\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct subsidies and grants for renewable energy research, development, and deployment have historically represented 15-20% of total energy R&D spending in OECD countries\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/publications/insights/RenewableEnergyIncentives\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Carbon pricing mechanisms such as emissions trading systems (ETS) and carbon taxes create economic advantages for renewable energy by increasing the operating costs of fossil fuel-based generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/publications/articles/CarbonPricing\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Renewable energy zones and spatial planning policies designate areas with favorable conditions and streamlined permitting for large-scale renewable projects, reducing development timelines and administrative costs\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2018/Sep/Renewable-Energy-Zones\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
