      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Geothermal energy\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal energy is thermal energy generated and stored within the Earth, originating from the radioactive decay of minerals and residual heat from planetary formation, with temperatures increasing approximately 25-30\u00b0C per kilometer of depth in the Earth's crust\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-geothermal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal power plants can operate with capacity factors of 70-90%, making them among the most reliable renewable energy sources, compared to solar (15-25%) and wind (35-45%)\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy13osti/56290.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global geothermal electricity generation capacity reached approximately 14 GW in 2020, with major producers including Iceland, New Zealand, the Philippines, Indonesia, and the United States\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021-Jun/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal heat pumps can achieve efficiency ratings of 3-6 COP (Coefficient of Performance), providing heating and cooling with significantly lower energy consumption than conventional systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/heat-pump-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS) involve artificially fracturing hot rock formations to create reservoirs for heat extraction, potentially expanding geothermal availability beyond traditional volcanic regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/what-are-enhanced-geothermal-systems-egs\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Conventional geothermal power generation requires specific geological conditions (high temperature gradients and accessible water/steam resources), limiting deployment to tectonically active regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/geothermal-direct-use-and-district-heating-cooling\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct-use applications of geothermal energy include district heating, greenhouse agriculture, and industrial processes, accounting for approximately 50% of global geothermal energy utilization\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iga.net/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental impacts of geothermal plants are generally minimal compared to fossil fuels, though concerns include induced seismicity from fluid injection, hydrogen sulfide emissions, and thermal impacts on local water resources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy12osti/55415.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Initial capital costs for geothermal power plants are high ($2-5 million per MW), but levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) is competitive at $60-100/MWh over plant lifetime\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2018-Jan/Renewable-cost-statistics-2018\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Iceland generates approximately 30% of its electricity from geothermal sources and utilizes geothermal energy for heating over 90% of buildings, demonstrating successful large-scale implementation in suitable regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nea.is/geothermal/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
