  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Ocean thermal energy conversion\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Ocean thermal energy conversion (OTEC) is a renewable energy technology that exploits the temperature difference between warm surface seawater and cold deep seawater to generate electricity.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/marine-and-hydrokinetic-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"OTEC systems require a minimum temperature difference of about 20\u00b0C (36\u00b0F) between surface and deep water to operate efficiently, limiting deployment to tropical and subtropical regions.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/highlights/otec.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The theoretical global OTEC potential is estimated at approximately 10 terawatts if developed in all suitable ocean regions.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2019/May/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2019\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"OTEC technology uses a closed Rankine cycle or open cycle system, where warm surface seawater heats a working fluid to create steam that drives turbines connected to generators.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1564999\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"As of 2024, there are no large-scale commercial OTEC plants operating globally, though several pilot projects have been developed in Hawaii, Japan, and the Indian Ocean.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/articles/ocean-thermal-energy-conversion-promising-renewable-resource\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Main challenges for OTEC commercialization include high capital costs, corrosion and biofouling issues in seawater systems, and the need for expensive deep-water pipes.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/highlights/otec.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"OTEC systems have the potential to produce fresh water as a byproduct through desalination, adding economic value to the technology.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/marine-and-hydrokinetic-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The efficiency of OTEC plants is typically between 2-4%, which is lower than conventional power plants but comparable to other ocean renewable technologies.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/highlights/otec.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
