      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Wind energy (onshore and offshore)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy is one of the fastest-growing renewable energy sources globally, with global wind capacity reaching over 1,000 GW by 2022 and continuing to expand at approximately 10-15% annually.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2023-Mar/Renewable-capacity-statistics-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind turbines typically have capacity factors of 35-45%, while offshore wind turbines achieve higher capacity factors of 50-60% due to more consistent wind resources at sea.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79752.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Modern wind turbines have experienced significant cost reductions, with onshore wind levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) declining by approximately 70% between 2010 and 2022.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2023-May/Renewable-Power-Generation-Costs-in-2022\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind technology has higher capital costs than onshore ($3,000-5,000/kW vs $1,000-1,500/kW) but benefits from stronger and more consistent wind resources and higher capacity factors.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/77714.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy integration into power grids requires complementary technologies including energy storage, grid modernization, and demand-side management to address intermittency challenges.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/75860.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind installations are primarily limited by suitable land availability, environmental impacts, and social acceptance, while offshore wind faces challenges with installation complexity, maintenance accessibility, and marine ecosystem impacts.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2211467X21001012\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"China leads global wind energy production with over 300 GW of installed capacity, followed by the United States and European countries, with significant growth expected in Asia and Latin America.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://gwec.net/global-wind-report-2023/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore floating wind turbines are emerging as a technology to access deep-water resources beyond fixed-foundation depth limitations (typically >60 meters), expanding deployment potential.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80576.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
