  "container": null,
  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydrogen fuel and fuel cells\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen fuel cells generate electricity through an electrochemical reaction between hydrogen and oxygen, producing only water and heat as byproducts, making them a zero-emission energy source.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/fuelcells/fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The most common type of fuel cell for transportation and stationary applications is the Proton Exchange Membrane (PEM) fuel cell, which operates at relatively low temperatures of 50-100\u00b0C.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.fuelcellsworks.com/news/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Currently, approximately 95% of hydrogen production comes from fossil fuel processing (steam methane reforming and coal gasification), while only about 5% comes from electrolysis using renewable energy.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Green hydrogen, produced through electrolysis powered by renewable electricity, is considered essential for achieving net-zero emissions in heavy industry, long-distance transport, and heating sectors.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Several countries including Japan, South Korea, Germany, and China have announced national hydrogen strategies targeting commercialization of fuel cell vehicles and infrastructure development.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.fuelcellsworks.com/news/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Toyota's Mirai and Hyundai's Nexo are among the few commercially available hydrogen fuel cell vehicles in production, with limited refueling infrastructure primarily in California, Japan, and parts of Europe.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/fuelcells/fuel-cell-vehicles\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The cost of hydrogen fuel cell systems has declined significantly over the past decade, from over $60/kW in 2006 to approximately $33/kW in 2020, with further cost reductions projected through mass production.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-and-fuel-cells-program\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Stationary fuel cells for backup power and combined heat and power systems have found early commercial deployment in hospitals, data centers, and industrial facilities.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.fuelcellsworks.com/news/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen storage remains a technical challenge, with methods including compressed gas storage (350-700 bar), liquid hydrogen storage at -253\u00b0C, and solid-state hydrogen storage materials still under development.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/fuelcells/fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The International Energy Agency projects that hydrogen could account for approximately 10% of global energy consumption by 2050 under ambitious clean energy scenarios.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
