{
  "id": "msg_011Cf7cE9VpQ8W6KPeUGipwB",
  "container": null,
  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Tidal and wave energy\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy is generated through the gravitational pull of the moon and sun on Earth's oceans, creating predictable and cyclical tidal movements that can be harnessed through underwater turbines and barrages\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2014/Ocean_Energy_Technology_Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy is derived from the kinetic and potential energy of surface waves, which are primarily generated by wind, and can be captured using various technologies including point absorbers, attenuators, and oscillating water columns\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy12osti/54667.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy has higher energy density and greater predictability compared to wave energy, with capacity factors often exceeding 50% in suitable locations, while wave energy capacity factors typically range from 25-50%\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S136403211730231X\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global tidal energy potential is estimated at approximately 1 TW, while wave energy potential is estimated between 1-10 TW depending on measurement methodology and geographic location\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2020/Dec/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current commercial tidal energy technologies include horizontal-axis and vertical-axis underwater turbines, while wave energy technologies are less commercially mature with limited operational installations worldwide\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/ocean-energy-technology-roadmap-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns for tidal and wave energy include impacts on marine ecosystems, fish and mammal migration, noise generation, and electromagnetic fields from subsea cables\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0960148117300733\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"As of 2023, tidal energy deployment is concentrated in regions such as the UK, France, Canada, and South Korea, with a global installed capacity of approximately 500-600 MW\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/renewable-energy-market-update-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy technology development remains largely in pilot and demonstration phases, with projects in Europe, North America, and Australia, but no significant commercial-scale deployment globally\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/77674.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Capital costs for tidal energy projects range from $2.5-6 million per MW, while wave energy projects typically have higher costs ranging from $3-10 million per MW, both significantly higher than mature renewable technologies\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.int/publications/2020/Jun/Renewable-Power-Generation-Costs-in-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal and wave energy technologies operate independently of weather patterns like solar and wind, though wave energy is still weather-dependent on different timescales, providing complementary renewable energy sources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032121000666\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
