  "container": null,
  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Carbon capture and utilization technologies\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Carbon capture and utilization (CCU) technologies can be categorized into three main stages: capture from point sources (industrial facilities, power plants) or direct air capture (DAC) from ambient air, followed by transport and storage, and utilization in products or permanent sequestration\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Post-combustion capture, pre-combustion capture, and oxy-fuel combustion are the primary carbon capture methods for point sources, while direct air capture uses sorbent or solvent-based technologies to extract CO2 directly from the atmosphere\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/direct-air-capture\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Captured CO2 can be utilized in enhanced oil recovery (EOR), chemical production (methanol, synthetic fuels), building materials (cement, concrete), beverages, and food processing, with varying levels of technical maturity and market viability\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.globalccsinstitute.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current global carbon capture capacity is approximately 40 megatons CO2 per year across operational facilities, with point-source capture being more cost-effective than direct air capture at current technology maturity levels\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/data-and-statistics/charts/global-direct-air-capture-capacity-and-projections\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of direct air capture currently ranges from $150-600 per ton of CO2, while point-source capture ranges from $50-100 per ton, with costs expected to decline through technological advancement and scale deployment\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/direct-air-capture\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CCU technologies contribute to climate mitigation primarily through permanent storage of CO2 in geological formations or long-lived products, though utilization in most commercial products results in eventual re-release of CO2 to the atmosphere\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Significant challenges for CCU deployment include high energy requirements, separation costs, long-term regulatory frameworks, monitoring and verification protocols, and the need for sustained CO2 demand in utilization markets\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.globalccsinstitute.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Policy support mechanisms for CCU include carbon pricing systems, investment tax credits, mandatory carbon reporting requirements, and government funding for research and demonstration projects, with the United States and European Union implementing the most comprehensive frameworks\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/carbon-capture-utilisation-and-storage-in-industrial-transitions\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
  "model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
