  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Geothermal energy\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal energy is derived from the thermal energy generated and stored within the Earth's interior, with the heat flow from the planet's core providing a continuous renewable energy source.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-geothermal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal power plants can operate with capacity factors of 70-90%, making them among the most reliable renewable energy sources compared to solar (15-25%) and wind (25-35%).\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The top geothermal energy producing countries are Iceland, New Zealand, Kenya, Indonesia, and the Philippines, with Iceland deriving approximately 30% of its electricity from geothermal sources.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/statistics\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal systems are classified into three main types: hydrothermal (steam and hot water), petrothermal (Enhanced Geothermal Systems or EGS), and geopressured resources.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal/types-geothermal-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct use applications of geothermal energy include district heating, greenhouse agriculture, and industrial processes, accounting for approximately 28 GW of installed capacity globally.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2020-May/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS) technology involves creating reservoirs in hot rock formations where water is injected to enable heat extraction in areas without natural hydrothermal resources.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal/enhanced-geothermal-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global geothermal electricity generation capacity reached approximately 14 GW in 2021, with an annual growth rate of 3-4% over the past decade.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/statistics\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal energy has minimal environmental impact compared to fossil fuels, with lifecycle greenhouse gas emissions ranging from 10-50 grams CO2-equivalent per kilowatt-hour.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The primary challenges for geothermal energy expansion include high upfront drilling costs, geological uncertainty, and limited availability of viable sites, restricting deployment to tectonically active regions.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Binary cycle geothermal plants use organic working fluids instead of water to generate electricity, enabling operation at lower temperatures and reducing environmental concerns related to fluid reinjection.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-geothermal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
